---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CtaBanner from "../components/CtaBanner.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";

const yearsActive = new Date().getFullYear() - site.founded;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`About ${site.name} — building SaaS tools and custom software since ${site.founded}. ${yearsActive}+ years, ${site.projectsDelivered}+ projects delivered.`}
>
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="hero-blob"></div>
    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-20 pb-10 sm:pt-24">
      <p class="text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-indigo-600">About us</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-slate-900">
        We build software <span class="gradient-text">small businesses</span> actually want to use.
      </h1>

      <!-- Credentials strip -->
      <div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-3 gap-4 max-w-2xl">
        <div>
          <p class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold gradient-text">{site.founded}</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500">Founded</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold gradient-text">{yearsActive}+</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500">Years of experience</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold gradient-text">{site.projectsDelivered}+</p>
          <p class="mt-1 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-500">Projects delivered</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Story -->
  <section class="pb-12">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 space-y-6 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-lg">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">The story</h2>
      <p>
        {site.name} is an independent software studio founded in {site.founded}. We started because we kept
        watching agencies, retailers and small teams stitch together half a dozen spreadsheets, free
        trials, and abandoned tools — and pay for the privilege. {yearsActive}+ years and
        {site.projectsDelivered}+ projects later, we still build the way we did on day one: opinionated
        stack, fast UI, real customer feedback.
      </p>
      <p>
        Most software for small businesses is built by one of two camps. The first is enterprise vendors
        who scale their product down — same complexity, same feature creep, same six-figure professional-services
        bill — just with a "small business edition" label. The second is shoestring SaaS startups whose
        product works fine for fifty customers and starts crumbling at five hundred. Neither serves the
        actual middle: a 5-30 person business that needs production-grade software but doesn't want to be
        billed like a hedge fund.
      </p>
      <p>
        That middle is who we build for. Our first product, <a class="text-indigo-600 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/products/travel-crm">TravelERP</a>,
        is a modern travel-agency CRM. It does the unglamorous, end-to-end work: capture leads, build
        multi-option quotes, share them publicly, convert to bookings, track installments, send hotel
        vouchers, and nudge customers via email and WhatsApp. It runs in production for travel agencies
        across India — small operators handling 50 leads a month, established agencies handling several
        hundred.
      </p>
      <p>
        We've followed it with <a class="text-indigo-600 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/products/ai-email-autoreply">AI Email AutoReply</a>
        (currently in private beta), and have two more products on the way:
        <a class="text-indigo-600 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/products/inventory-pro">Inventory Pro</a>
        for retailers and <a class="text-indigo-600 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/products/hr-hub">HR Hub</a>
        for people-ops teams. All built on the same opinionated foundation: modern stack, fast UI,
        multi-tenant from day one, API-first, secure by default.
      </p>
      <p>
        Alongside the products, we take on <a class="text-indigo-600 font-semibold hover:underline" href="/services">custom-build engagements</a>
        — AI chatbots, web apps, mobile apps — for teams that need bespoke software shipped fast. Most
        years, services revenue is what funds product development. That arrangement keeps us close to
        real customer problems and prevents us from designing products in a vacuum.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Values -->
  <section class="py-16 bg-slate-50 dot-bg">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight text-center">What we believe</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-slate-600 text-center">
        Six things we keep coming back to when we make decisions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 mt-10 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6">
        <p class="text-3xl">🛠</p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 font-bold text-slate-900">Ship often, ship small</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
          We release every week. Small releases are easier to reason about, easier to roll back, and
          easier for customers to consume. Quarterly mega-releases are how organisations hide that nothing
          actually got shipped.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6">
        <p class="text-3xl">🎯</p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 font-bold text-slate-900">Each product does one job well</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
          The temptation to bolt a "marketing automation module" onto a CRM, or a "POS module" onto an
          HRMS, is endless. We resist it. A product that does one thing brilliantly beats a product that
          does fifty things badly every time.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6">
        <p class="text-3xl">🤝</p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 font-bold text-slate-900">Customers shape the roadmap</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
          We talk to every customer who'll talk to us. Pricing changes, feature decisions, design choices
          — they're informed by what real users tell us in real calls, not by what we think they want
          based on a survey.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6">
        <p class="text-3xl">🔒</p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 font-bold text-slate-900">Multi-tenant by default</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
          Every product is built multi-tenant from the first line of code. Each customer gets isolated
          data — proper isolation, not "best efforts". Going multi-tenant after the fact is one of the
          hardest re-architectures in SaaS; we'd rather pay the upfront cost.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6">
        <p class="text-3xl">📖</p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 font-bold text-slate-900">No vendor lock-in</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
          For services projects, your code lives in your GitHub from day one. For products, your data is
          exportable at any time in standard formats. Both decisions cost us short-term leverage and earn
          us long-term trust.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6">
        <p class="text-3xl">💬</p>
        <h3 class="mt-2 font-bold text-slate-900">Honest sales</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
          If our product doesn't fit your use case, we say so on the first call. If a freelancer would
          do a better job at half the price, we recommend a freelancer. The sales conversations we lose
          this way are the ones that would have ended in refunds anyway.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How we work -->
  <section class="py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">How we work</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed">
        Some practical details about how we actually run the studio.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 space-y-6 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Remote-first, India-based</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">
            We're headquartered in India and work remotely. That gives us reach across IST business hours
            and US/EU overlap. We meet clients in person in NCR / Gurugram when it matters, but most
            engagements run on calls, async written updates, and Friday demos.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Modern, boring stack</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Our products and most service projects use ASP.NET Core or Node.js on the backend, React
            and Next.js on the web, React Native and Flutter on mobile. PostgreSQL or SQL Server for
            relational data; we add Redis or pgvector when there's a real reason. We use boring tech on
            purpose — fewer surprises in production, easier to hand off, easier to hire for.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Fixed-price engagements</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Service work is quoted upfront after a 30-minute discovery call. The proposal locks scope and
            timeline and we eat the cost of anything we agreed to and missed. Material scope changes go
            through a written change order with a delta price — but the default is to absorb small
            additions inside the spirit of the original agreement.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Weekly Friday demos</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Every project gets a working build deployed to staging every Friday. You see real progress,
            not slide decks, and you steer the build week by week. Bad ideas die early; good ones get
            polished.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Documentation and handoff</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">
            For services projects, we ship the codebase with architecture documentation, environment
            setup, and a one-page operations runbook. For products, we maintain public release notes,
            a help centre, and respond to support requests within one business day on all paid plans.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900">Post-launch support</h3>
          <p class="mt-2">
            Every services project includes a 30-day post-launch warranty: anything we shipped that
            breaks within the first month is on us to fix at no charge. Beyond that, ongoing maintenance
            is available on a retainer scoped to your actual needs — or you can take full ownership of
            the codebase and run it yourself.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Team -->
  <section class="py-16 bg-slate-50">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">The team</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed">
        {site.name} is a small core team supplemented by senior contractors we've worked with for years.
        We deliberately stay small because every additional layer of management dilutes the quality of
        the engineering. Our engineers talk to customers directly; our designers ship code; our founders
        still answer support tickets.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
        If you want to know who specifically will work on your project — we'll tell you on the call.
        Same names, same code review process, same QA standards across every engagement. No "expert team"
        on the sales call and offshore juniors on the build. What you see is what you get.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Quick FAQ -->
  <section class="py-16 sm:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight">Quick answers</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed">
        Common questions about working with us. There's a fuller <a href="/faq" class="text-indigo-600 font-semibold hover:underline">FAQ here</a> if you want detail.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-10 space-y-3">
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between font-semibold text-slate-900">
            Are you an agency or a SaaS company?
            <svg class="shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            Both. We run our own SaaS products on monthly subscriptions, and we take on fixed-price
            custom builds — AI chatbots, web apps, mobile apps. Many clients use both: subscribe to
            TravelERP, and hire us to integrate it with their other systems.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between font-semibold text-slate-900">
            Do you work with clients outside India?
            <svg class="shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            Yes. We have clients across India, the Middle East, Singapore and the UK. Billing in INR
            with GST for Indian customers; USD or EUR for everyone else. Time zone overlap is sufficient
            for daily standups across India, the Middle East and Europe; for North America we run async
            communication and one weekly call.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between font-semibold text-slate-900">
            How long does a typical project take?
            <svg class="shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            AI chatbots: 3–6 weeks. Web apps: 6–12 weeks. Mobile apps: 8–12 weeks. These are honest
            ranges for projects of typical scope — bigger or more complex builds take longer, smaller
            ones less. We send a quote with a real timeline within 48 hours of the discovery call.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between font-semibold text-slate-900">
            Can we own the code?
            <svg class="shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            For services projects: yes, fully. Code lives in your GitHub from day one of build. The IP
            transfers to you on final payment. No reserved licenses, no "core platform" we lock you out
            of, no usage royalties.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center justify-between font-semibold text-slate-900">
            What do you not do?
            <svg class="shrink-0 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
            We don't do pure marketing or branding work (logos, brand books). We don't do video
            production. We don't do social media management. We don't do anything blockchain-related.
            We're a software studio — we build software, and we say no to work outside that lane.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBanner />
</BaseLayout>
